landing-review: vulkan-only plan, landing 10c-2 -- the input module splits on the packet

Range reviewed: main...HEAD (1e576f6..0470c2b, 5 commits).
Reviewer: dedicated read-only agent, `high` (CLAUDE.md: "changes the
synchronisation between the game thread and the render thread").
THREE rounds -- the budget CLAUDE.md allows -- because round 1's HIGH and
round 2's findings were each fixed with NEW code in the same class.

WHAT LANDED. tagpu_input.c was SPLIT, not moved. Its token half
(tagpu_input_frame: tagpu_keys.txt, the injected pointer, the shield's
held-modifier expiries) reads no frame packet, so it joined the trigger
family on the game thread. Its camera hold became tagpu_input_eye_frame
and stayed in tagpu_overlay_draw on the render thread, where f->packet
exists and where its answer is read back.

THE PLAN PRESCRIBED A DIFFERENT FIX AND IT WAS WRONG. It called for a
read-only tagpu_packet_pub_last() so do_eye could run on the game
thread. Every bullet of that design is true; the conclusion is not. It
reasons entirely about the PRODUCER and never asks who reads do_eye's
OUTPUT: s_eyeHold/s_holdX/s_holdY/s_holdValid are read by
tagpu_input_cmd, whose only caller is tagpu_zoom_frame_end, which runs
only inside tagpu_overlay_draw. The accessor would have made four words
cross-thread to serve a consumer that is not on the other side -- and
delivered nothing on gdi anyway, since the hold reaches the game thread
through tagpu_cmd_post, also called only from the overlay frame.
A move is decided by what reads the code being moved, not by what the
code being moved reads.

VERIFIED BY RUNNING IT, three configurations:
  gdi, bare (tagpu_defaults.off, no tagpu_gui.on):
      "gui: trigger host only ..."; ui answers; click 187 403 takes
      MAINMENU.GUI -> SINGLE.GUI; keys esc comes back.
  vulkan, armed: "gui: ARMED flip@0x4C63A0=1 leaves=17/17"; click drives.
  vulkan, armed, live game: scenario load drove the menus BY CLICK into
      Two Continents and applied 5 of 5; the camera hold is exact
      (eye == the point asked for, twice) and --release genuinely frees
      it (the camera edge-scrolled 1200 -> 1904 -> 6704 and stopped when
      the pointer left the edge); the UI layer is working end to end
      (gui: mm=0x0/0 refused=0 pic=252x252/0, cursor: owned=207797).
  viol=0 pviol=0 foreign=0 throughout; no ErrorLog.txt.

ROUND 1 -- one HIGH, real, mine, and SILENT.
  H1  The trigger host was gated on a play default. before_flip has one
      installer, tagpu_gui_init, whose first line was
      `if (!read_tokens()) return;` -- tagpu_gui.on. tagpu_opt_read
      answers -1 for an absent file once tagpu_defaults.off exists, and
      `tacli launch` writes that unless given --defaults. So on an
      ordinary instance `tacli click` wrote the token file, printed
      "sent:", and nothing read it -- ON EVERY RENDERER, because the
      render-thread call this landing deleted did not consult that
      trigger. tacli also arms the shield by default, so hardware input
      was blocked on the same window: drivable by nothing, with no line
      in the log, the early return being above the only glog().
      My own verification had passed because it used --defaults. The
      dependency is invisible in the passing case.
      FIXED (3120504): the observer installs whenever the flip's bytes
      match; tagpu_gui.on gates the UI layer, the census and the 17
      leaves alone. Two wins fall out -- a build whose LEAF prologues
      differ no longer costs us the flip, and `tacli gui remove` stops
      removing the ability to drive the instance. Every exit logs now.
  M1  "tagpu_apply_patches() applies three patches with no runtime gate,
      and the tagpu_curs pair" triples the ungated count and counts the
      pair twice: 0x4266A7 is ungated, 0x43E50C and 0x499041 are both
      inside the else of a tagpu_curs.off test. It also contradicted the
      same page's correct table two sections below. That number is
      landing 11's exit-condition arithmetic.
  Four LOWs and two NITs, all applied.

ROUND 2 -- no new HIGH. It verified the fix is complete (still the only
installer; DllMain calls it unconditionally; the chain rule means nobody
else can take 0x4C63A0) and that the newly-hooked configuration cannot
change the rendered frame.
  Four findings, ONE cause: installing the observer without the leaves
  left the UI layer's op machinery running with nothing to feed it.
  surf_of_ctx REGISTERS a surface and only before_memfree (leaf #15)
  RETIRES one -- a registrar split from its destructor. ops_window_reset
  memsets 4 KB on EVERY flip there. surf_of_ctx dereferences four dwords
  behind ptr_ok ALONE, and what underwrote that was the 17-site byte
  match, not the flip's six generic prologue bytes -- CLAUDE.md is
  explicit that ptr_ok is never the safety argument. And gui.on=census
  on a build with mismatching leaves would have written tagpu.log at up
  to 200 Hz for the life of the process.
  FIXED (07eb652) with one line: `if (!s_opsLive) return 0;` after the
  trigger block. s_opsLive rather than s_installed on purpose -- a
  PARTIAL leaf install leaves s_installed 0 with detours live.
  M1  The skill's own new diagnostic rule would have cost someone a
      working instance: it said "if neither line is in tagpu.log, no
      tacli verb can answer", but two of the three exits it ignored are
      DRIVABLE. It is a table now, keyed on the phrase both undrivable
      lines carry.
  M2  tagpu_shield.h asserted "nothing here requires either thread"
      while pointing at the .c note that refutes it: tagpu_shield_frame
      calls clear_state() on the disarm edge, memsetting the wndproc's
      per-key arrays.

ROUND 3 -- NO HIGH, NO MEDIUM. It cleared the control flow in detail:
`return 0` and `return hijack` are the same expression at that point;
the detour stub's codegen (test eax,eax / jz +8) is what makes
after_flip never run for a declined flip; and objdump shows DDRAW.dll
first in TotalA.exe's import table, so attach runs under the loader lock
before the EXE entry point and the install window is provably flip-free.
  Eight findings, every one prose. Zero added lines with a semicolon or
  brace. Two were my own new text and both would have misled: I claimed
  the ARMED configuration does not pay the per-flip memset (it does,
  under gui.on=off and on armed+gdi, where g_gui_draw is never set), and
  I called util_pull_messages' rate limit inert because the assignment
  inside that function is commented out -- last_msg_pull_tick is stamped
  on nearly every message the game pulls, so that conjunct is the
  STRONGEST of the five. Same error shape as the do_eye one: reading the
  code in front of me instead of grepping for who else writes it.

NOT CLOSED BY THIS LANDING.
  - tacli roster and tacli scenario load still do not work on gdi. The
    live-map signal is `units: alive=`, emitted by log_units, which is
    in tagpu_overlay_draw. Only the DETECTOR fails: the game really does
    load, proven by running `scenario apply` against an instance that
    `scenario load` had just declared dead -- applied 5 of 5. That is
    landing 10c-3, so gate 10c is three landings.
  - AND 10c-3 CANNOT RIDE ON THE PACKET. tagpu_packet_acquire has two
    call sites, render_vk.c and render_ogl.c; gdi has no consumer, so
    the FRESH gate blocks every non-forced publish and fill_frame runs
    about once per level. A roster hung off fill_frame or a pub_last()
    accessor would produce nothing on the one lane it is for. It has to
    walk the unit array on the game thread, inside a publisher file.
  - tacli eye and tacli wheel still do not reach gdi, for two different
    mechanisms with one cause (no overlay frame): the hold has no
    command record, the wheel refuses earlier at !s_live.
  - renderer=gdi is still not stock AS A LANE: one ungated patch
    (0x4266A7) plus the tagpu_curs pair behind a file. Landing 11's.
  - The 4 KB per-flip memset is still paid by armed+gdi and by
    gui.on=off. Pre-existing; newly interesting now that gdi is the
    stock lane.
  - gui-renderer.md's parity md5 row was measured when "trigger absent"
    meant NO detour on 0x4C63A0. It now means an observer plus the
    trigger family every 16 ms. With 07eb652's early return that
    configuration touches nothing below the trigger block, so the
    argument is stronger than before -- but it is an argument, not a
    re-measurement.

